---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { SITE, PRICES } from '../consts';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Hog Trapping Service', path: '/hog-trapping-service' },
  { name: 'Hog Damage Prevention', path: '/hog-damage-prevention' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Feral Hog Damage Prevention for Texas Land | Red Creek Outdoors"
  description="How to read feral hog sign, what the damage costs, and when trapping the whole sounder beats waiting. For Central Texas landowners, sourced to Texas A&M."
  crumbs={crumbs}
>
  <PageHero eyebrow="Landowners" title="Stopping Feral Hog Damage" crumbs={crumbs}
    lede="You can't fence your way out of a hog problem on open ground, and you can't wait it out. Here's how to read what's happening and when to call." />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>What it costs</h2>
      <p>
        A 2004 Texas A&M AgriLife Extension survey put feral hog damage to Texas agriculture at $52 million a year, and the
        Texas A&M Natural Resources Institute estimates the state carries around 3 million wild pigs. Those numbers are
        old and the herd has only grown. On a single property the cost shows up as torn-up pasture, hay you can't cut,
        ruined food plots, busted fence and muddied stock tanks.
      </p>

      <h2>Read the sign</h2>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li><strong>Rooting.</strong> Ground turned over like a bad disc job, often along creek bottoms, fence lines and low wet spots.</li>
        <li><strong>Wallows.</strong> Mud holes at tanks, seeps and creek edges, usually with crusted mud rubbed on nearby trees and posts.</li>
        <li><strong>Rubs.</strong> Mud and hair on posts, poles and tree trunks at hog height.</li>
        <li><strong>Trails.</strong> Low tunnels through brush and under fences, used the same way every night.</li>
        <li><strong>Tracks.</strong> Rounder and blunter than deer tracks, often in groups.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What works and what doesn't</h2>
      <p>
        Hogs breed faster than casual pressure can keep up with. AgriLife Extension estimates up to 66 percent of a
        population has to come off every year just to hold it level. Shooting the odd hog at the feeder doesn't get close,
        and it teaches the survivors to come in later and leave sooner.
      </p>
      <p>
        What does work is taking whole sounders, sows included, in single events, then keeping pressure on what's left.
        That's the trapping job. For the boars that won't trap, night hunts with thermal finish it.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Hog trapping {PRICES.trapping.text.toLowerCase()}.</strong>{' '}
          <a href="/hog-trapping-service">See how a trapping job runs</a>, or call{' '}
          <a href={`tel:${SITE.phoneE164}`}>{SITE.phone}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>While you wait for us</h2>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>Don't shoot at the sounder. Every miss and every single kill makes the rest harder to trap.</li>
        <li>Note where and when you see fresh sign. Mornings after rain are the easiest time to read it.</li>
        <li>Stop any deer corn that's getting hit at night. It's feeding the hogs on your dime.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="sources">
        Sources:{' '}
        <a href="https://nri.tamu.edu/news/2019/november/startling-statistics-everyone-needs-to-know-about-wild-pigs/">Texas A&M Natural Resources Institute</a>;{' '}
        <a href="https://agrilifeextension.tamu.edu/asset-external/feral-hog-population-growth-density-and-harvest-in-texas/">Texas A&M AgriLife Extension, Feral Hog Population Growth, Density and Harvest in Texas</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand title="Seeing sign?" text="Tell us where and how fresh. We'll come read it with you." />
</Base>
